<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rock of Cashel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cashel of the Kings</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rock of Cashel is a limestone outcrop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a>, Ireland, that was the seat of the kings of Munster from about the 4th century. In 1101 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-ua-briain">Muirchertach Ua Briain</a> gave it to the Church. Its buildings include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cormacs-chapel">Cormac's Chapel</a>, a Romanesque church consecrated in 1134, a round tower and a 13th-century cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rock-of-cashel.jpg" alt="Rock of Cashel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_of_Cashel_(49163525453).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Rock of Cashel, also called Carraig Phádraig or St. Patrick's Rock, rises above a plain at Cashel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a>. Local legend says the Rock fell there from the Devil's Bit, a mountain 30 kilometres to the north, when Saint Patrick banished Satan from a cave. According to the Tripartite Life of Saint Patrick, Cashel is the reputed site of the conversion of the King of Munster by Patrick in the 5th century. The whole plateau on which the buildings and graveyard stand is walled, and the graveyard holds a number of high crosses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Rock was the traditional seat of the kings of Munster as early as the 4th century, before the Norman invasion. In the 5th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a> built a fortress at Cashel and kept their supremacy there for hundreds of years. In 977 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a> was crowned king there and made Cashel his capital, and in 1101 the King of Munster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-ua-briain">Muirchertach Ua Briain</a>, gave his fortress on the Rock to the Church. Few remnants of the early structures survive; most buildings on the site date from the 12th and 13th centuries.</p><p>In 1647, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-confederate-wars">Irish Confederate Wars</a>, Cashel was sacked by English Parliamentarian troops under Murrough O'Brien, Earl of Inchiquin. The Irish Confederate troops there were massacred, as were Catholic clergy including Theobald Stapleton, and many religious artefacts were looted or destroyed. During or after the mid-1730s the Anglican Archbishop of Cashel, Arthur Price, had the cathedral roof removed, a decision that was criticised then and since. In 1763 a large number of Whiteboys levelled the wall surrounding the Rock, during a period of their activity in Tipperary between 1763 and 1765.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The round tower, about 28 metres high, is the oldest and tallest structure and dates from about 1100. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cormacs-chapel">Cormac's Chapel</a> was begun in 1127 and consecrated in 1134. It has vaulted ceilings, carved tympana, an arcaded interior and exterior, the oldest stairs in Ireland and the only surviving Romanesque frescoes in Ireland. Twin towers at the junction of nave and chancel suggest German influence, and the Irish abbot Dirmicius of Regensburg sent two carpenters to help with the work. The sandstone has become waterlogged over the centuries, so the chapel has been enclosed in a rain-proof structure with dehumidifiers.</p><p>The cathedral, built between 1235 and 1270, is an aisleless cruciform building with a central tower and a massive residential tower house at the west end. The 15th-century Hall of the Vicars Choral holds St. Patrick's Cross and was restored by the Office of Public Works in 1975; visitors now enter the site through it. Scully's Cross, put up in 1860, was destroyed in 1976 when lightning struck a metal rod running the length of the cross.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rock of Cashel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the early medieval period the Érainn had been eclipsed by the Eóganachta, who built the Kingdom of Munster around <b>Cashel</b>. Érainn descendants such as the Corcu Loigde survived as subordinate kingdoms in west Cork.</blockquote><a href="/state/iverni">Iverni</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Glanmire in 1118 divided Munster, giving the south, Deasmumu, to the MacCarthys, heirs of the Eoganacht kings of <b>Cashel</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-desmond">Kingdom of Desmond</a></li><li><blockquote>From around the 5th century the Eóganachta dynasties, tracing descent from Conall Corc, the legendary founder of <b>Cashel</b>, established an over-kingship over the province, displacing earlier Érainn peoples such as the Corcu Loigde.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Kingdom of Munster</a></li></ul></section>
